edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163079 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162113 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163080 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163079 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163080 #edb/ref 1061 38093 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163080 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ccc00629-902d-501f-86c2-7072c1aad6a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163080 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162114 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163086 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163070 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163086 #edb/ref 1061 38149 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163086 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2f745e7f-72c3-508a-96ad-ef71ea659d96" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163086 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163333 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163087 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163071 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163087 #edb/ref 1061 38150 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163087 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c79d4341-23ea-55d9-84aa-e073291790d1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163087 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163334 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163111 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163115 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163111 #edb/ref 1061 38977 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163111 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6c5298f8-ee76-510b-9852-47db3df79bd2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163111 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162132 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163113 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163008 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163113 #edb/ref 1061 39007 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163113 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f84ee295-698e-570e-8b1d-f95c9890f4ba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163113 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162134 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163114 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163111 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163114 #edb/ref 1061 39008 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163114 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d02007b7-e508-588a-8b76-1a1887b8fb3d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163114 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162135 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163115 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163116 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163115 #edb/ref 1061 39009 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163115 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "54ee6fbf-8ad8-5c2d-b013-e251b532c2d5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163115 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163343 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163116 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163009 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163116 #edb/ref 1061 39010 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163116 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2be6a036-867e-5d15-93fd-01acf1d032ce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163116 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162136 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163290 #edb/ref 1064 "We often want to measure the strength of the (linear) dependence between two random variables. One measure that we use for this purpose is covariance. \n\nThe **covariance** between two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y] = \\textrm{E}\\Big[\\left(X - \\textrm{E}[X]\\right)\\left(Y - \\textrm{E}[Y]\\right) \\Big].\n$$\n\nLet's break this formula down a bit:\n\n- $\\left(X - \\textrm{E}[X]\\right)$ is the deviation of $X$ from its mean\n\n- $\\left(Y - \\textrm{E}[Y]\\right)$ is the deviation of $Y$ from its mean\n\nTherefore, the covariance measures the expected value of the product of these deviations.\n\nThe covariance can also be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y] =\\sum_{(x,y)\\in S}(x − \\textrm E[X])(y − \\textrm E[Y])f(x,y)\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint PMF of $X$ and $Y,$ and $S$ is the joint support.\n\nLet's sketch a few diagrams to get a feel for what the covariance tells us.\n\n- A *positive* covariance means that if $X$ increases, then $Y$ is likely to increase. We say that $X$ and $Y$ are **positively correlated**.\n\n![](images/bb/bbfae3f0fb38bc8652cd26716e4b0b128cf1f2039350267034d97161d7f5dac4.png)\n\n- A *negative* covariance means that if $X$ increases, then $Y$ is likely to decrease. We say that $X$ and $Y$ are **negatively correlated**.\n\n![](images/fd/fd45053fdc39f070e3438c3b8c61fe01613201595cfe00bd4cc6c6e481bf73ab.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If $X$ and $Y$ are independent, then \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y] = 0.\n$$\n\nThe reverse implication does not always hold! For example, two random variables can have zero covariance yet be dependent. This is because covariance measures *linear* dependence only.\n\n- The covariance of a random variable with itself is its variance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,X] & =\\text{E}[(X-\\text{E}[X])(X-\\text{E}[X])] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{(X-\\text{E}[X])}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\text{Var}[X]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Aside from indicating whether two random variables are positively or negatively correlated, the covariance *by itself* does not tell us very much. For example, if \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X_1, Y_1] > \\textrm{Cov}[X_2, Y_2] > 0\n$$\n\nthis does not necessarily mean that the dependence between $X_1$ and $Y_1$ is somehow \"stronger\" than the dependence between $X_2$ and $Y_2.$ This is because the covariance does not take the variance of the individual random variables into account. \n\nIn a future lesson, we'll use the covariance to define the so-called  **correlation coefficient,** which *does* consider the variance of the individual random variables and so *can* be used to compare the relative strength of the linear dependence between two pairs of random variables." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163290 #edb/ref 1065 5624 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163290 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "aa0d2bd6-235b-5d66-abc0-b8869af0ae0e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163290 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163291 #edb/ref 1064 "We can easily extend the notion of conditional probability distributions for discrete random variables to continuous random variables.\n\nFor continuous random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the **conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y = y$** is given by\n\n$$\nf_{X|Y} (x|y) =\\dfrac{f(x, y)}{f_Y (y)}, \\qquad f_Y(y) \\neq 0,\n$$\n\nand the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X = x$ is given by\n\n$$\nf_{Y|X} (y|x) =\\dfrac{f(x, y)}{f_X (x)}, \\qquad f_X(x) \\neq 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that these definitions are analogous to those for discrete random variables.\n\nTo compute probabilities, we work directly with these conditional PDFs. For example, to calculate the **conditional probability of $X$ given $Y,$** we use\n\n$$\nP(X \\in A|Y = y) =\\int_A f_{X|Y} (x|y)\\, \\textrm dx.\n$$\n\nIn general, the conditional distribution of $X$ given $Y$ does not equal the conditional distribution of $Y$  given $X$. That is\n\n$$\nf_{X|Y}(x|y)  \\ne f_{Y|X}(y|x).\n$$\n\nFinally, if $X$  and $Y$  are independent, then\n\n$$\nf_{X|Y}(x|y)=f_X(x),\\qquad f_{Y|X}(y|x)=f_Y(y).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163291 #edb/ref 1065 5625 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163291 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4e66c559-ad43-5152-a04e-7aa8b01374ef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163291 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163292 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's now prove the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y] =\\textrm E [XY] − \\textrm E[X]\\cdot \\textrm E[Y]\n$$\n\nWe start with the definition of covariance, given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y] =\\textrm  E\\Big[\\left(X− \\textrm E[X]\\right)\\left(Y−\\textrm E[Y]\\right)\\Big].\n$$\n\nExpanding the product in the brackets, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY-X\\cdot \\text{E}[Y]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot Y+\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y]].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that if $a$ and $b$ are constants, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[a]=a, \\qquad \\textrm{E}[aX + bY] = a\\cdot \\textrm{E}[X] + b \\cdot \\textrm{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nSince $\\textrm{E}[X]$ and are $\\textrm{E}[Y]$ are both constants, the covariance formula simplifies as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY-X\\cdot \\text{E}[Y]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot Y+\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y]] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X\\cdot \\text{E}[Y]]-\\text{E}[\\text{E}[X]\\cdot Y]+\\text{E}[\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y]] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y]+\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y]\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163292 #edb/ref 1065 5635 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163292 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "57fd5059-dc1e-5473-9f12-db4e68dc88af" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163292 #edb/ref 1067 "Proof of the Covariance Formula" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163293 #edb/ref 1064 "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, the covariance between $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y] =\\iint_\\limits{\\mathbb R^2}(x − \\textrm E[X])(y − \\textrm E[Y])f(x,y) \\, \\textrm d x\\textrm d y.\n$$\n\nAs is the case with discrete random variables, it's often easier to compute the covariance using the following formula: \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y]\n=\n\\textrm{E} [XY] - \\textrm{E}[X]\\cdot \\textrm{E}[Y]\n$$\n\nSuppose we're given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n![](images/c5/c589b62f579cb5efe7c4a2b86aa9bebbbbec81517c8bd7872c3c6a0b019e08cc.svg)\n\nTo compute the covariance of this distribution, we first use the rule of the lazy statistician to find $\\textrm{E}[XY]\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}xyf(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}xy\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(x+\\frac{y}{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{2}y{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{2}y{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}y]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{2}y[\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{4}y]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{1}{6}y+\\frac{1}{8}{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{12}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{24}{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot {2}^{2}+\\frac{1}{24}\\cdot {2}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing methods discussed in previous lessons, we can compute $\\textrm{E}[X]$ and $\\textrm{E}[Y]{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\dfrac{7}{12}, \\qquad \\textrm{E}[Y] = \\dfrac76\n$$\n\nFinally, using the covariance formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}-(\\frac{7}{12})(\\frac{7}{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{72}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember that if two random variables are independent then their covariance equals zero. Let's see an example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163293 #edb/ref 1065 5637 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163293 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "066864ea-8aaa-5094-bf78-7c862b1dd64e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163293 #edb/ref 1067 "Covariance for Continuous Random Variables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163308 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's consider the least-squares problem $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b},$ where \n\n![](images/4d/4d538e6a37eccc1f49f34d3aa1b31a0539d50b351e6029595af1b1f7e0f2967a.svg)\n\nRecall that the corresponding least-squares solution is a vector ${\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\Vert  A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} - \\mathbf{b} \\Vert\n$$\n\nis as small as possible.\n\nIn real-world problems, the coefficients stored in $A$ might come from sources that are not equally reliable. For example, the coefficients might be collected from population surveys with different sample sizes. As a result, we might want to assign a greater \"weight\" to the more reliable data.\n\nFor example, suppose we require the weight of the coefficients in the first row to be twice the weights of the other rows. Then, we can re-state our problem by saying that, for the matrix of weights $W,$ given by\n\n![](images/72/7268fdd7c8701a9cf780342d6eab11015d7764694d17c28a2fae2336db267223.svg)\n\nwe wish to find vectors ${\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\Vert  W (A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} - \\mathbf{b}) \\Vert\n$$\n\nis as small as possible.\n\nTherefore, instead of solving $A\\mathbf x = \\mathbf{b},$ we now need to solve the least-squares problem \n\n$$\nW\\!A\\mathbf x = W\\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nTo get the normal equation corresponding to this least-squares problem, we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\!A)^T{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n![](images/0c/0c7459270d9d27c40bdf5b0e6dc7eb0c7e17951541b77936804a9057f488c13f.svg)\n\nthe normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}36 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}9 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}72 \\\\ -54\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the final equation, we obtain the least-squares solution ![](images/72/7259d4c16fa297be7b02cab3ed48a848d1fa73de61c2f14351885072c93c6362.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186163308 #edb/ref 1065 6076 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163308 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "92c4b629-686d-5377-b646-ffe0dd0d636a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163308 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163312 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that if the solution ${\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ of the least-squares problem $A\\mathbf x=\\mathbf{b}$ is unique, then $A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ lies in $\\textrm{Col}(A).$ Furthermore, $A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ is the vector in $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ that's closest to $\\mathbf b.$ \n\nIn terms of vector spaces, the vector $A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ is the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{b}$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of $A.$ Therefore, the error vector $A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} - \\mathbf{b}$ is orthogonal to $\\textrm{Col}(A).$\n\n![](images/3a/3a1c79036954f0e04212892b7e3d79c75f8cd7b81d7785441f090f89a4eb6faf.png)\n\nA similar property holds for weighted least-squares solutions:\n\n*If the solution ${\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ of the least-squares problem $W\\!A\\mathbf x=W\\mathbf{b}$ is unique, then\n$A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} - \\mathbf{b}$ is orthogonal to $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ with respect to the weighted dot product defined as \n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle_W = \\sum_{i=1}^n w_i^2 x_i y_i,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the $w_i$'s are the weights from the main diagonal of the weight matrix $W.$* \n\nWe previously considered the weighted least-squares problem $W\\!Ax=W\\mathbf{b},$ where\n\n![](images/d4/d44c48deb0864fb8fd6aa92ce8af408b600de8abf2039a3f3cd7397fabc7d9fe.svg)\n\nIt's easy to show (using the usual techniques) that the unique weighted least-squares solution is given by \n\n![](images/db/db8f62fcb085d8c1fcfc22bb53cb8f22bd9a65b211e5fd5d4017e30bf9bdb4c7.svg)\n\nComputing our error vector $\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon} = A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} - \\mathbf{b},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ε & =A\\hat{x}-b \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 14\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let $\\mathbf a_1$ and $\\mathbf a_2$ denote the columns of $A\\mathbin{:}$ \n![](images/3f/3fbafcf3f9d80d2d54089647b2f0239662dcbe56c9ba68539e2303ce752d28ee.svg)\n\nNotice that the error vector $\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon}$ is *not* orthogonal to the columns of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- Computing $\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon} \\cdot \\mathbf a_1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ε\\cdot {a}_{1} & =⟨\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =(1)(1)+(4)(1)+(-4)(2) \\\\  & =-3 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Computing $\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon} \\cdot \\mathbf a_2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ε\\cdot {a}_{2} & =⟨\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =(1)(-1)+(4)(0)+(-4)(-1) \\\\  & =3 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, if we check the orthogonality with respect to the *weighted* dot product \n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle_W = \\sum_{i=1}^n w_i^2 x_i y_i = 4x_1y_1 + x_2y_2 + x_3y_3,\n\\quad\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{⟨ε,{a}_{1}⟩}_{W} & ={⟨\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}⟩}_{W} \\\\  & =4(1)(1)+(4)(1)+(-4)(2) \\\\  & =0\\,✓ \\\\ {⟨ε,{a}_{2}⟩}_{W} & ={⟨\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}⟩}_{W} \\\\  & =4(1)(-1)+(4)(0)+(-4)(-1) \\\\  & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} - \\mathbf{b}$ is orthogonal to $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ with respect to the weighted dot product. We can write this as\n\n$$\nA{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} - \\mathbf{b} \\perp_W \\textrm{Col}(A).\n$$\n\nIn terms of vector spaces, the vector $A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ is the orthogonal projection with respect to $\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle_W$ of the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of $A.$ \n\nFurthermore, the weighted least-squares problem with weight matrix $W$ is equivalent to finding a vector ${\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ such that\n\n$$\n\\Vert  A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} - \\mathbf{b} \\Vert_W\n$$\n\nis as small as possible." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186163312 #edb/ref 1065 6084 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163312 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "91e0f414-7785-5748-bba9-04278c6519ad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163312 #edb/ref 1067 "A Geometric Interpretation of the Weighted Least-Squares Solution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163326 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu$ and variance $\\sigma^2,$ then the sample mean, denoted $\\overline{X},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} = \\dfrac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i.\n$$\n\nThe sample mean is a random variable whose probability distribution (also called the **sampling distribution**) has an expected value of $\\mu\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\overline{X}] = \\mu\n$$\n\n \n\nIn other words, the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is an unbiased estimate of the population mean $\\mu.$\n\nLet's now find the variance of the sampling distribution of $\\overline{X}.$ Using the properties of variance for sums of independent random variables, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}] & =\\text{Var}[\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}] \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot \\text{Var}[{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{n}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot (\\text{Var}[{X}_{1}]+\\text{Var}[{X}_{2}]+⋯+\\text{Var}[{X}_{n}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot ({\\sigma }^{2}+{\\sigma }^{2}+⋯+{\\sigma }^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot n{\\sigma }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo summarize, we have the following important result:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[\\overline{X}] = \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}\n$$\n\nIt should be noted that we don't yet know the sampling distribution of $\\overline{X}.$ What we *do* know is that whatever the sampling distribution of $\\overline{X}$ is, it has mean $\\mu$ and variance $\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163326 #edb/ref 1065 6276 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163326 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "58763c8a-23b3-55bb-9b5c-4781a3c271b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163326 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163333 #edb/ref 1064 "The standard deviation of the sample mean is called the **standard error** of the sample mean and is denoted $\\textrm{SE}[\\overline{X}]\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\overline{X}] = \\sqrt{\\textrm{Var}[\\overline{X}]}\n$$\n\nSo, if we have a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu$ and variance $\\sigma^2,$ then the  standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\overline{X}] = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}} = \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt n}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163333 #edb/ref 1065 6300 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163333 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c2217692-f38f-597c-9295-cd25f1d4d026" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163333 #edb/ref 1067 "The Standard Error" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163334 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we have a random sample ![](images/ff/ff40ddf134336ef15bcaf81b5b23aa8fb7c5ea13276e0f86fa6dde15841e0c5d.svg) of size ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) from a population with mean ![](images/75/75d2faaecd7646e08d87276399a471f8f713fbeda95e358e94410ac2e23b42a5.svg) and variance ![](images/1a/1af459c3f0307649888db8b148eef968a9c5da6a77788c4ab7fe32d6ea30717e.svg) We know that the sample mean \n\n![](images/51/513b88bd8fa93336cc949758da79cac238f871b477d1682ab6b20635a4149361.svg)\n\nis an unbiased estimator of ![](images/98/9878f65a2daf16e1fbd2b0c4929e4407c4ddc406c147dab8a5f6b1a8ee25af33.svg) \n\nHowever, it should be noted that selecting a single observation ![](images/3c/3c8b064868e0622effd6b83427f453ecdb3e033319c9e02969bb988b22ec7cb6.svg) to represent the whole could *also* be considered an unbiased estimator of the population mean. Recall that, by assumption\n\n![](images/e7/e7cc0631f5e6b060f0b9fc77dcac428c2b853d0e19be2e9b20e6e9f8d5d7aaad.svg)\n\nIn what sense is ![](images/84/84269b1ed65c2a169ac4246065d27cbba714010309ddb03a65f7fa878b7b65ea.svg) a \"better\" estimator of ![](images/75/75d2faaecd7646e08d87276399a471f8f713fbeda95e358e94410ac2e23b42a5.svg) than, say, ![](images/c3/c3bb2b6f3769acd8b9c9ce9f929b5bc6c7ec840ab18503cf4c878a1fe8f8f41d.svg) The answer lies in their respective variances. \n\nAs the sample size ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) becomes larger and larger, the variance and standard error of ![](images/84/84269b1ed65c2a169ac4246065d27cbba714010309ddb03a65f7fa878b7b65ea.svg) become smaller and smaller. It is easy to see that, as ![](images/f4/f4d85c2aac179db9322aef2478cfa080e9d602f8aeb8c0436f646f0e2321c139.svg)\n we have\n![](images/d5/d5676c70b4fcb7f68704ba0bb46fc262c7d8f764748e252aa989d6e82e88283b.svg)\n\nSo, when the sample size ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) is large, there is less variance in the sampling distribution of ![](images/dc/dc01b2699b68bb8be585039efc249eaeeba5e4ad17a5c687a8811bf266cabc87.svg) Therefore, the larger the sample, the more likely it is that ![](images/84/84269b1ed65c2a169ac4246065d27cbba714010309ddb03a65f7fa878b7b65ea.svg) will give good estimates for ![](images/98/9878f65a2daf16e1fbd2b0c4929e4407c4ddc406c147dab8a5f6b1a8ee25af33.svg) The individual sample elements do not have this property since ![](images/63/631e2156cfdd9541694e1238fb8c717e721e0ef452de4bb898beb2c4509abc6b.svg)\n\nThe key takeaway is that the sample mean ![](images/84/84269b1ed65c2a169ac4246065d27cbba714010309ddb03a65f7fa878b7b65ea.svg) becomes a better estimator for the corresponding population mean ![](images/75/75d2faaecd7646e08d87276399a471f8f713fbeda95e358e94410ac2e23b42a5.svg) as the sample size ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) increases." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163334 #edb/ref 1065 6301 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163334 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "2fc54f89-7780-558c-8193-28d92a025bc8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163334 #edb/ref 1067 "The Sample Mean and Large Samples" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163343 #edb/ref 1064 "You may have noticed that computing the covariance using the definition is quite a lot of work. Is there a formula that makes it easier?  \n\nIndeed, there is. It can be shown that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y] =\\textrm E [XY] − \\textrm E [X]\\cdot \\textrm E [Y].\n$$\n\nWe'll prove this formula at the end of the lesson. But for now, let's get some practice at applying it." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163343 #edb/ref 1065 6455 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163343 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "8bb09eee-27a4-5c3e-b10d-923dbaa105df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163343 #edb/ref 1067 "A Formula for Quickly Computing Covariance" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163900 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186163902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163900 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186163904 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163900 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186480836 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163900 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186481015 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163900 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186481048 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163900 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163900 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163900 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186481015 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163900 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a885e1f2-0379-5509-836d-b0ab6acba537" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163902 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{4}{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163902 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 1759218604